  <!-- SECTION 2: FLINT VS DENT HETEROSIS & COLD ADAPTATION -->
  <section id="flints-heterosis" aria-labelledby="flints-heterosis-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="flints-heterosis-title">Heterosis</h2></div>
    </div>

<div class="flints-heterosis-grid">
      <article class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <h3>Post-Columbian Introduction &amp; Lineage Divergence</h3>
        <p>
          Following multiple introduction waves from the Caribbean and North America in the 16th century, European maize established a distinct flint germplasm pool. Through centuries of regional selection in Central and Northern Europe, flint landraces adapted to shorter growing seasons and cooler temperatures, accumulating extensive chromosomal and retrotransposon differences from American Dent germplasm.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <h3>Genomic Basis of Dent &times; Flint Heterosis</h3>
        <p>
          Hybrid maize breeding in Europe fundamentally exploits the strong heterotic pool complementation between European Flint and US Dent \(such as B73 and Mo17\). Comparative genomics reveals that gene copy number variations, presence/absence variations \(PAVs\), and non-overlapping retrotransposon insertions between flint and dent genomes drive hybrid vigor in F1 progeny.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <h3>Cold Tolerance &amp; Early Growth Vigor</h3>
        <p>
          Flint inbreds exhibit superior germination, seedling emergence, and photosynthetic capacity under low spring temperatures \(10–14&deg;C\). Assemblies of DK105, EP1, F7, and PE0075 uncover duplications in gene networks governing cold-stress signaling, chloroplast stability, anthocyanin biosynthesis, and rapid cell division during juvenile growth.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: KEY PUBLICATIONS & ABSTRACT PREVIEWS -->
